<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gangga Negara</h1><p class="st-meta">200 CE – 1025 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A half-legendary Hindu-Buddhist kingdom in the Beruas and Dinding area of Perak, remembered as one of the oldest polities of the Malay Peninsula.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gangga Negara is known mainly from Malay tradition, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-merong-mahawangsa">Hikayat Merong Mahawangsa</a>, and from scattered archaeological finds around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beruas">Beruas</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perak-sultanate">Perak</a>. Its name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for 'city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>', points to the Indian cultural influence that reached the peninsula's west coast in the early centuries CE. No inscription confirms a founding date.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Finds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beruas">Beruas</a> and nearby sites, including Buddhist statues, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and Chinese ceramics, suggest a riverine trading settlement exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and forest products. Little is known of its government beyond what later legends say.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tradition holds that the kingdom was destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-invasion-of-srivijaya">Chola raids</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola</a> around 1025. The later Malay state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beruas">Beruas</a>, which flourished before the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perak-sultanate">Perak Sultanate</a>, is often linked with its memory.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gangga-negara.jpg" alt="Gangga Negara" width="320" height="344"><figcaption>Image: Lilauid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Malaya_circa_1922_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 CE – 1025 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gangganegara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=612&amp;focus=gangga-negara" aria-label="Gangga Negara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7841 2083 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gangga Negara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7187 2016h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8161 2339h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8161 2339h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Malaysia. <a href="/atlas?year=612&amp;focus=gangga-negara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

